ref 1080 "40m/s" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062190 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062190 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6a085e2e-5ee8-5c7b-8f03-c4b35261d43e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062923 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062923 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062923 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062923 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062923 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186062928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062923 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062923 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062923 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1e13f342-74ba-5f29-a5e3-5849701e2a61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062924 #edb/ref 1080 "2x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062924 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062924 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cd8407c1-3175-5166-ab8e-c0f6c6813aad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062925 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062925 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062925 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e1308e12-f501-5153-88f2-76e216435b39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062926 #edb/ref 1080 "4x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062926 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062926 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0456556b-527c-530f-bae6-45f5a5491b9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062927 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{10}{3x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062927 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062927 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "368933d9-de3c-5973-9020-320c9eb737b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062928 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5}{x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062928 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062928 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4fbe0d2f-31b8-54e9-b7b0-4f96a8068d4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064016 #edb/ref 1069 "e-218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064016 #edb/ref 1071 "Each side of a square is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min}.$ How fast is the area of the square decreasing when the sides are $10\\,\\text{cm}?$\n\n![](images/a0/a06eb0963dc6d01e45acb9349e5232ba70a11c98c99ee63c2ec0dae3d748f5ed.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064016 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec1aaa71-efe4-50fd-8552-bcc98abbd3a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064016 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} .\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA = x^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min},$ so we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} = -2.$ Also, when the sides are $10\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $x=10.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =(-2)\\cdot 2(10)=-40{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square decreases at a rate of $40\\,\\text{cm}^2/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064017 #edb/ref 1069 "e-219" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064017 #edb/ref 1071 "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $20 \\text{ cm}^2\\text{/s}.$ If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square respectively, then find an expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064017 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8780fe5c-94c5-5ac6-b6f6-6e86df29b416" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064017 #edb/ref 1074 "This situation is different. Here, the *area* is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/f4/f4125cebd7553bb8b106a8baff39a6e4a120b9d9e5cdf2c4c9c0a9a676558897.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA = x^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-20\\text{ cm[math]^2[/math]/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-20 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{10}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064018 #edb/ref 1069 "e-220" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064018 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/26/2677121139896da73eca5277c7c23f193467001eabba976cb48b2232f4bcbad6.png)\n\nIn a testing facility, a scientist is increasing the diagonals of a simple square at a rate of $12 \\text{ mm}\\text{/min}.$ At what rate, in squared millimeters per minute, is the area of the square increasing when the length of the diagonal is $6\\, \\text{mm}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064018 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6264adc4-c5ab-5b84-b89f-27b4382baa0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064018 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ increases at a rate of $12\\,\\textrm{mm/min},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=6.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}A}{\\textrm d t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =12\\cdot 6=72{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064019 #edb/ref 1069 "e-221" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064019 #edb/ref 1071 "The sides of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to twice the rate of increase of the diagonal. What is the area of the square at that moment?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064019 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7187c402-9949-5671-af33-8929bc5344a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064019 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing implicit differentiation and the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 2\\dfrac{\\textrm d h}{\\textrm d t}$, so replacing $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm dt}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 2 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have $h=2,$ and therefore the area of the square at that moment is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{2^2}{2} = 2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064372 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064372 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064372 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064372 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064372 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064372 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064372 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064372 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064372 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064372 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064372 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Area Given the Rate of Change of the Side Length" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064372 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064372 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a61a3e76-4936-5976-abf0-b9b485c4434d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064373 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064373 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064373 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064373 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064373 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064373 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064373 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064373 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding an Expression for the Rate of Change of a Side Length Given the Rate of Change of the Area" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064373 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064373 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2edbb40a-1d59-5c75-bcc0-93584ec9c030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064374 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064374 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064374 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064374 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064374 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064374 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064374 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064374 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating a Rate of Change of Area Given a Rate of Change of a Diagonal: Word Problem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064374 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064374 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2e2af960-841b-56a9-bc7c-099bf82e0fe8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064375 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064375 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064375 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064375 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064375 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064375 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064375 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064375 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064375 #edb/ref 1039 "Relating the Rate of Change of a Diagonal Length to Another Rate of Change" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064375 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064375 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating Related Rates With Squares" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186065307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064454 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "82288cda-51b8-5251-be5d-9081504538b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064688 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186062185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064688 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2770" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064688 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cc/cc27a817900f1e7bef61f73da6bbdb3aaae4e30d59490dce4395416d98f4edae.png)\n\nThe sides of a square increase in length at a rate of $2m/s$. At what rate is the area of the square increasing when the sides are $10m$ long?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064688 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "82bcc198-7907-5fda-ad7c-ebdd316cd930" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064688 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064811 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186062923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064811 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48339" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064811 #edb/ref 1071 "The area of a square increases at a rate of $10in^{2}/hour$. If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square respectively, then find an expression for $\\frac{dx}{dt}$, where $t$ is the time in hours." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064811 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d6bf115-1a99-51dd-8a30-6ca9b66d4cb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065307 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065307 #edb/ref 1061 886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065307 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ffbfe0ce-ca07-507a-ba31-6478157ea3cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065307 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065308 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065308 #edb/ref 1061 887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065308 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c09954da-b118-515b-b58d-96db87ec4eef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065308 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065309 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065309 #edb/ref 1061 890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065309 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9506770d-7ccc-51f6-a401-872306dd8258" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065309 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065310 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065310 #edb/ref 1061 892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065310 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ffbba909-2219-5660-9dd2-f5e847787776" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065310 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065311 #edb/ref 1061 893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065311 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "04a14c93-d5fa-5e41-a14a-97df92f692d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065311 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065312 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065312 #edb/ref 1061 895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065312 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "df2d4588-710d-52c2-96f4-192142c85391" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065312 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065380 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that the lengths of the sides of a square are growing at a **rate** of $2\\,\\textrm{cm}$ every second, as shown below.\n\n![](images/6e/6e791c7c33636ef3b2b798fde58825cf4cf1f76e3b6abdd2d92317aa555e1420.png)\n\nIf the square is growing, then its area is also increasing. Our question is, \"*what is the rate at which the area of the square is increasing*?\" In other words, if $A$ is the area of the square at time $t$, then what is\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}?$$\n\nLet $x$ be the length of the side of the square. We know that the relationship between the area $A$ and side length $x$ is given by\n\n$$A = x^2.$$\n\nUsing implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe're told that $x$ grows at a **rate** of $2\\,\\textrm{cm/s},$ so we have\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} = 2.$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t},$ we get\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 2 \\cdot 2x =4x.$$\n\nAnd that's it. We're done!\n\nIf we want to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ at a particular moment, we substitute the value of $x$ at that moment into the above.\n\nFor example, to work out the rate of change of area when $t=2$ seconds, we substitute the corresponding value of $x$ into the formula. When $t=2$ we have $x=4$ (from the diagram) and therefore\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 4(4) = 16\\,\\textrm{cm}^2/\\textrm{s}.$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065380 #edb/ref 1065 478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065380 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "28860e65-09c5-5b95-a728-4cef6731b8b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065380 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065382 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that the rate of change of the diagonal of a square is known, and we're interested in finding the rate of change of some other quantity. For example, can we calculate the rate of change of the area of a square given the rate of change of its diagonal?\n\nBefore we get into that, let's recall a few facts about squares. Consider the square $ABCD$ with sides of length $x$ and diagonals of length $h.$\n\n![](images/0c/0cc3f0cd81cae3bab92ec6c3826f64b7e54e0af61cc32a000569afe5171a27dc.png)\n\nUsing the Pythagorean Theorem, we know that\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & ={h}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & ={h}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe also know that the area $A$ of a square is given by $A = x^2.$ So, since $x^2 = \\dfrac{h^2}{2},$ we have\n\n$$A = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.$$\n\nThis formula will prove particularly handy in the next example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065382 #edb/ref 1065 481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065382 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ae7e4021-bb7a-5e1e-a370-ff99a10aa8a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065382 #edb/ref 1067 "Rates of Change Involving the Diagonals of a Square" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371590 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371590 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115930" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371590 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371590 #edb/ref 1071 "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $56{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate are the lengths of the sides of the square decreasing when the sides are $8\\text{ m}$ long?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371590 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "332ede8a-b7d8-56ae-b05d-98854e11eb9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371590 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/1c/1cb323f4c38bb4fc13ffc81f47544424872480b42f033032da47c04d097e8696.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-56\\text{ m}{}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-56 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{28}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when the sides are $8\\,\\text{m},$ we have $x=8.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{28}{8}=-\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the lengths of the sides are decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371591 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371591 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371591 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371591 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371591 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371591 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371591 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371591 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186371593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371591 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c59fb865-44ed-5cc4-bf4d-8ff775b26354" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371592 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371592 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371592 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fad524f3-747b-543d-877c-19d0bc18ff69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371593 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371593 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371593 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d1d8801c-f0ce-5902-a2aa-2522226312ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371594 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{m/s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371594 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371594 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5f70dac8-847a-5a3c-afa9-5b5ad69de949" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371595 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{7}{4}\\,\\text{m/s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371595 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371595 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2c00a4c0-f033-55e1-9d1b-40d157b2fcff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371596 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{m/s}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371596 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371596 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e6aeaa69-cb2d-572c-ad4b-0228aa3f284a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372540 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372540 #edb/ref 1069 "q-314495" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372540 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372540 #edb/ref 1071 "The sides (in feet) of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to seven times the rate of increase of the diagonals. What is the length $h$ of the diagonal of the square at that moment?\n\n$h=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372540 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d06153ec-06de-5e08-a5e0-36bc3ef9e7e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372540 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=7\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$, so replacing $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 7\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 7 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have that the length of the diagonals of the square is $7\\,\\text{ ft}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372541 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372541 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372541 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372541 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186372542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372541 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6262049f-56fe-5753-8d3a-24cb4987048f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372542 #edb/ref 1080 "7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372542 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372542 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "66ffdd0c-16fb-5f50-b5ba-1cb07f13ccce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372899 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372899 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344507" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372899 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372899 #edb/ref 1071 "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $30{\\text{ in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$ Given that $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square, respectively, find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in minutes, as a function of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372899 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "175199e1-96a5-594b-b670-66eb928d5ac0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372899 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/a4/a42b7ac5c9f5870d256b6474c9b95730d32184ba22ca80235c76ffe0ff346f31.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-30\\text{ }{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-30 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{15}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372900 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372900 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372900 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372900 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186372901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372900 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ab6ddcc7-548b-528c-b8e3-9ff38fb06abf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372901 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{15}{x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372901 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372901 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "030b95f8-281c-522a-9c71-ee2a31868eaf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372905 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372905 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344614" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372905 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372905 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6f/6f440744e4dd65883ae2ae564595491b36c1371a553c2052d2923f8f617caaf2.png)\n\nA manufacturing process gradually reduces the size of a square opening by shortening its diagonals at a rate of $2.2\\text{ mm}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the opening decreasing when each diagonal measures $10\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe area is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372905 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d743fe47-5489-54f0-9f74-3b3ae11c1658" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372905 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $h$ be the length of the diagonals and $A$ be the area of the square. Using the Pythagorean theorem, the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ decreases at a rate of $2.2\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-2.2.\n$$\n\nWhen the diagonal length is $h=10,$ substituting into the expression above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=(-2.2)(10)=-22{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square opening is decreasing at a rate of $22{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372906 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372906 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372906 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372906 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186372907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372906 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "240a125a-7965-503b-98eb-0c6543b7dbff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372907 #edb/ref 1080 "22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372907 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372907 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a1297eee-ae31-559a-8063-29c28074e2db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398137 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398137 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116051" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398137 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398137 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/30/30b101fa9812c49237ab72c0c4aebd1666df6de80367eb2ee59b33fe0e422d87.png)\n\nIf the diagonals of a square decrease at a rate of $3\\sqrt{2}\\text{ m}\\text{/min}$, at what rate is the perimeter decreasing?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398137 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c40d4d8-93cd-5fd9-9c9b-8b9eaffb8fe0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398137 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is decreasing at a rate of $3\\sqrt{2}\\text{ m}\\text{/min},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =(-3\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-12\\text{ m/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square decreases at $12\\text{ m}/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398138 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398138 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398138 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398138 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398138 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398138 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398138 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398138 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186398142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398138 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "015670c7-de29-5d58-b9b4-055602c56ffa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398139 #edb/ref 1080 "24\\text{ m}/\\text{min}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398139 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398139 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "daf51f91-0a6d-569d-8c03-c177edaa1dc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398140 #edb/ref 1080 "10\\text{ m}/\\text{min}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398140 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398140 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ad1ee6ea-b061-51dc-b501-839195b28399" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398141 #edb/ref 1080 "6\\text{ m}/\\text{min}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398141 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398141 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6d4f5765-990d-5307-bcda-feb606bb929b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398142 #edb/ref 1080 "12\\text{ m}/\\text{min}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398142 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398142 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "58cdd8af-ecc0-5a53-9201-682ba5b6106b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398143 #edb/ref 1080 "3\\text{ m}/\\text{min}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398143 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398143 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d97728d8-7183-59a2-bbab-433924b0cf9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398855 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398855 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48372" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398855 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398855 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4f/4f636c252da8e886c1f4bf9f0d87387339fce91e5a72f6275e6b24bba787d288.png)\n\nAnna is an environmentalist and is studying the impact of deforestation in a pine forest located in a square-shaped region. Anna discovers that the area of ​​the deforested region is decreasing at a rate of $0.4{\\text{ km}}^{2}/\\text{month}.$ At what rate are the region's diagonals decreasing if the diagonals are $20\\text{ km}$ long?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398855 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "51c2bb08-d00d-5a09-b26c-2e5b5f8107fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398855 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $A$ decreases at a rate of $0.4{\\text{ km}}^{2}/\\text{month},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=20.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{20}\\cdot (-0.4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{0.4}{20} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.02\\,\\text{km}\\text{/month}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the diagonals of the region are decreasing at a rate of  $0.02\\text{ km}/\\text{month}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398856 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398856 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398856 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398856 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398856 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398856 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398856 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398856 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 1759218639886